.TH ALOHA-OE 6 "October 13, 2026" "aloha-oe" "Games Manual"
.SH NAME
aloha-oe \- synthesize and play Aloha 'Oe
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aloha-oe
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aloha-oe
plays an arrangement of Queen Liliuokalani's Aloha 'Oe, with timing and
instrumentation based in part on Johnny Cash's recording.
The score and synthesis parameters are compiled into the program.
No sound samples, external score files, or network services are used.
.PP
The default arrangement is instrumental, in E major and 4/4 time.
It lasts about 180 seconds and includes guitar, bass, and slide guitar.
A synthetic singing voice, piano, and percussion are also available.
The voice is a small formant synthesizer; its pronunciation and likeness
to the recorded singer are approximate.
.PP
The program renders stereo PCM in memory before playback.
It can also write a RIFF/WAVE file or separate instrument tracks.
Exporting a file does not disable playback; use
.B \-n
for an unattended render.
.SH OPTIONS
Short options may be combined.
Long options accept either
.BI \-\-option= argument
or
.BI "\-\-option " argument .
There are no positional arguments.
When a setting is repeated, its last value is used.
.TP
.BR \-h , " \-\-help"
Print a summary of the options and exit.
.TP
.BI "\-o, \-\-wav " file
Write the rendered mix as stereo, signed 16-bit little-endian PCM
in a RIFF/WAVE file.
An existing file is replaced.
The name
.B \-
is treated as an ordinary filename.
.TP
.BR \-n , " \-\-no-play"
Do not open an audio device.
Rendering and requested exports still take place.
.TP
.BI "\-s, \-\-section " name
Select
.BR full ,
.BR verse ,
or
.BR chorus .
The default is
.BR full .
.TP
.BI "\-b, \-\-bpm " number
Set the score's quarter-note tempo, from 25 through 150 beats per minute.
The default is 49, corresponding to an eighth-note pulse of 98.
Setting this option to 98 doubles the default speed.
Fractional values are accepted.
.TP
.BI "\-t, \-\-transpose " semitones
Transpose the arrangement by an integer from \-12 through 12,
relative to E major.
The default is zero.
.TP
.BI "\-v, \-\-volume " number
Set the normalized peak amplitude, from 0 through 0.9.
The default is 0.85.
This changes the rendered signal, not the system volume.
.TP
.BI "\-r, \-\-rate " rate
Use a sample rate of 22050, 44100, or 48000 Hz.
The default is 48000.
The audio device must accept the selected format and rate.
.TP
.BI "\-\-seconds " number
Limit output to the given number of seconds, from 0.05 through 1200.
The output is shortened if the selected section ends first.
A short fade is applied at the end of a preview.
.TP
.BI "\-\-offset " number
Begin output this many seconds into the selected section.
The value must be from 0 through 1200 and less than the section's duration.
Earlier sound is rendered internally to preserve held notes and reverb;
a short fade is applied at the new beginning.
.TP
.B \-\-dry
Disable the generated room reverb.
.TP
.B \-\-lead-only
Omit the accompaniment and instrumental transitions.
Retain the score melody in the selected mode.
.TP
.BI "\-m, \-\-mode " name
Select
.BR instrumental ,
.BR vocal ,
or
.BR ensemble .
The default is
.BR instrumental .
In vocal mode the synthesizer sings the melody over the accompaniment,
with slide guitar retained for instrumental passages.
Ensemble mode adds a quieter slide-guitar melody alongside the voice.
.TP
.BR \-V , " \-\-voice"
Equivalent to
.BR "\-\-mode vocal" .
.TP
.BR \-e , " \-\-ensemble"
Equivalent to
.BR "\-\-mode ensemble" .
.TP
.BR \-l , " \-\-lyrics"
Write the lyrics to standard output during playback, letter by letter
as each syllable is sung.
Output is flushed after every letter.
This is enabled by default in vocal and ensemble modes when both the
vocal gain and master volume are nonzero.
It may also be enabled in instrumental mode.
.TP
.B \-\-no-lyrics
Suppress lyric output.
.TP
.BI "\-\-voice-style " name
Select
.B baritone
(the default) or
.BR retro .
Retro mode limits the voice bandwidth and applies approximately
11025 Hz sample holding and 7-bit quantization.
The accompaniment retains its normal precision.
.TP
.BI "\-\-ipa " text
Sing a phrase given in the supported subset of UTF-8 IPA.
This replaces the song with a single vocal note on E3,
subject to
.BR \-\-transpose .
The default duration is six seconds; use
.B \-\-seconds
to change it.
The text is a phonetic transcription, not English text to be converted
into speech.
Unsupported symbols and malformed UTF-8 are rejected.
The supported symbols are listed in doc/NOTES in the source distribution.
.TP
.BI "\-\-guitar-gain " number
Set the guitar gain, from 0 through 2.
The default is 1.
.TP
.BI "\-\-bass-gain " number
Set the bass gain, from 0 through 2.
The default is 1.
.TP
.BI "\-\-steel-gain " number
Set the slide-guitar gain, from 0 through 2.
The default is 1.
.TP
.BI "\-\-piano-gain " number
Set the piano gain, from 0 through 2.
The default is 0.
.TP
.BI "\-\-percussion-gain " number
Set the percussion gain, from 0 through 2.
The default is 0.
.TP
.BI "\-\-vocal-gain " number
Set the voice gain, from 0 through 2.
The default is 1.
This option does not enable a vocal mode by itself.
.TP
.BI "\-\-stems " directory
Write six WAVE files named
.BR guitar.wav ,
.BR bass.wav ,
.BR steel.wav ,
.BR piano.wav ,
.BR percussion.wav ,
and
.BR vocal.wav .
Create the directory if necessary; its parent must already exist.
Existing files with these names are replaced.
All tracks and the mix use a shared master gain, preserving their relative
levels and leaving disabled parts silent.
The largest peak among the mix and tracks determines this gain, so a mix
exported with stems can be quieter than one exported on its own.
.TP
.BI "\-\-score " file
Write scheduled note events as CSV, including note times, pitches,
levels, panning, and articulation parameters.
The export covers the whole selected section, regardless of
.B \-\-offset
or
.BR \-\-seconds .
An existing file is replaced.
.TP
.BI "\-d, \-\-device " name
Select the native output device; see
.B DEVICES
below.
The word
.B default
or an empty name selects the platform default.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Run the built-in score, tuning, timing, and signal-bounds checks, then exit.
These checks use fixed reference settings and do not open an audio device.
Other rendering options have no effect on the checks.
.SH LYRICS
Lyric timing follows playback progress reported by the audio backend.
It does not follow the time at which samples are submitted to a buffer.
Timing resolution and downstream latency depend on the device and backend.
NetBSD reports track consumption before downstream mixing and hardware
buffering; some Windows drivers permit only buffer-completion updates.
.PP
Each syllable's letters appear one by one from its onset, over at most
half of its sung length.
On a colour terminal, each phrase is first drawn in grey shortly before
it is sung, and its letters are highlighted as they are reached.
Otherwise, including when standard output is not a terminal, letters
are only appended as they are sung and text not yet sung is not shown.
Colour is also disabled by a nonempty
.B NO_COLOR
environment variable,
.BR TERM=dumb ,
or a terminal narrower than the longest phrase.
A newline ends each phrase.
Passages of four seconds or more without singing are marked with a
\(lq\[u266A] instrumental \[u266A]\(rq line, whose letters advance evenly
through the passage and finish two seconds before singing resumes.
Seeking into a phrase shows what has already been sung in it (without
colour, from the start of the current word); completed phrases are
skipped.
No lyrics are printed with
.B \-\-no-play
or during a standalone
.B \-\-ipa
audition.
Messages about rendering and playback go to standard error.
.SH DEVICES
The device argument is platform-specific.
There is no device-listing option.
.TP
.B Darwin (macOS)
A CoreAudio device UID.
Omitting the argument uses the default output through AudioQueue.
.TP
.B FreeBSD and DragonFly BSD
An OSS device pathname; the default is
.BR /dev/dsp .
.TP
.B Linux
An ALSA kernel PCM playback node.
The default is the first playback device listed in
.I /proc/asound/pcm
that is not an HDMI or DisplayPort output, or failing that the first
playback device, or
.BR /dev/snd/pcmC0D0p .
ALSA library names such as
.B hw:0
and plugin configuration files are not interpreted.
The device must support stereo S16_LE at the requested rate, with
5--40 ms periods and a 50--250 ms buffer.
.TP
.B Windows
A nonnegative decimal waveOut device number, such as
.BR 0 .
The default uses
.BR WAVE_MAPPER .
.TP
.B NetBSD 9 and later
An audio device pathname; the default is
.BR /dev/audio .
.TP
.B OpenBSD
An audio device pathname; the default is
.BR /dev/audio0 .
.PP
The native Unix backends require permission to open the device.
Direct hardware access, particularly on Linux and OpenBSD, may conflict
with a running sound server.
The program does not stop other audio clients or provide software mixing
or resampling for these devices.
Unsupported formats and underruns may cause playback to fail.
WAVE export remains available with
.BR \-\-no-play .
.SH EXAMPLES
Play a short chorus with voice and instruments:
.PP
.nf
    aloha-oe -e -s chorus --seconds 24
.fi
.PP
Write an instrumental arrangement without opening an audio device:
.PP
.nf
    aloha-oe -n -o aloha.wav
.fi
.PP
Export the mix and separate tracks from a passage:
.PP
.nf
    aloha-oe -ne --offset 48 --seconds 28 \\
        --stems tracks -o mix.wav
.fi
.PP
Audition a phrase using only the ASCII portion of the IPA alphabet:
.PP
.nf
    aloha-oe --ipa 'a lo ha oe' --voice-style retro
.fi
.PP
Play through the first Linux hardware PCM device at 44100 Hz:
.PP
.nf
    aloha-oe -r 44100 -d /dev/snd/pcmC0D0p
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success.
A status of 2 indicates an invalid option, an invalid IPA phrase, or an
invalid preview position.
A status of 1 indicates a rendering, export, playback, or check failure.
.SH FILES
No configuration files or runtime data files are required.
The program does not read recorded audio or MIDI files.
.SH NOTES
The default tempo is a measured approximation to the recording.
The arrangement does not reproduce every ornament or local change of tempo.
The vocal synthesizer is deliberately small and has limited intelligibility.
.PP
A full render at 48000 Hz needs about 69 MB for its stereo floating-point
sample buffer; stem export needs additional working storage.
.PP
Builds, checks, and playback have been tested on macOS (arm64).
The other native backends have not yet been tested on their target systems.
.PP
On Windows, the MinGW-w64 entry point converts command-line arguments
to UTF-8 and restores the console output code page on normal return.
File access still uses narrow-character C interfaces; use ASCII export
pathnames.
.SH SEE ALSO
The README and INSTALL files in the source distribution,
doc/NOTES for the arrangement and phoneme inventory,
doc/REFERENCES for source material,
and doc/PORTING for backend requirements and the PCM interface.
